WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.460
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.180
Our last class showed how a magnetic field turns a current loop.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.500
Today, we use that turning effect to measure a small steady current.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.840
A moving coil galvanometer contains a light coil with many turns, a pointer,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.460
a magnetic system and a restoring suspension or spring.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.540
Current produces a magnetic torque.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.099
The spring resists rotation, and the pointer stops where those torques

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:25.540
balance.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.500
The magnetic system is designed carefully.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.500
Curved pole pieces and a soft iron core produce an approximately radial field

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.460
in the working region.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.680
As the coil turns, the local field stays in its plane, perpendicular to the

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.160
coil's normal.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.200
The sine factor in the magnetic torque is therefore one throughout the useful

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.020
deflection range.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.712
This field is not uniform in direction across the instrument; we use the

00:00:50.712 --> 00:00:51.040
radial

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.460
field model with approximately constant magnitude at the active coil sides.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.060
Magnetic torque is N I A B.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.100
If c is restoring torque per radian, spring torque magnitude is c times theta.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.960
At equilibrium, c theta equals N I A B.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:14.320
So theta equals N A B over c, multiplied by current.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.160
Double the current within the operating range and the

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:21.700
deflection doubles. Notice the two different angles.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.660
The pointer's deflection changes, while the normal to local field angle stays

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.520
ninety degrees.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.180
Confusing them would incorrectly insert sine of pointer deflection into the

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:30.920
instrument equation.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.640
Damping helps the pointer settle; our equation describes its final steady

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.456
reading, not every instant during motion.

00:01:36.456 --> 00:01:38.196
How responsive is the pointer?

00:01:39.156 --> 00:01:43.476
We define current sensitivity here as deflection divided by current.

00:01:44.456 --> 00:01:49.796
It is N A B over c, measured in radians per ampere if we use angular units.

00:01:51.056 --> 00:01:55.636
Larger turn count, larger area or stronger field increases it when the other

00:01:55.636 --> 00:01:56.436
quantities are fixed.

00:01:57.156 --> 00:01:59.836
A softer restoring spring also increases it.

00:02:00.596 --> 00:02:05.076
Take one hundred turns, area two square centimeters, field zero point two

00:02:05.076 --> 00:02:10.172
tesla, and restoring constant four times ten to the minus six newton meters

00:02:10.172 --> 00:02:10.376
per

00:02:10.376 --> 00:02:10.596
radian.

00:02:11.716 --> 00:02:15.476
Two square centimeters is two times ten to the minus four square meters.

00:02:15.476 --> 00:02:20.756
N A B is zero point zero zero four, giving current sensitivity one thousand

00:02:20.756 --> 00:02:21.696
radians per ampere.

00:02:22.396 --> 00:02:26.701
A half milliampere produces zero point five radians of deflection in this

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:26.996
ideal

00:02:26.996 --> 00:02:27.596
linear model.

00:02:28.136 --> 00:02:31.216
Now let the bare coil resistance G be one hundred ohms.

00:02:31.616 --> 00:02:33.516
Its voltage is current times G.

00:02:34.636 --> 00:02:38.636
Voltage sensitivity is therefore current sensitivity divided by G, or ten

00:02:38.636 --> 00:02:39.376
radians per volt.

00:02:39.916 --> 00:02:43.676
That half milliampere needs zero point zero five volts across the coil.

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:46.636
Here is a useful exam trap.

00:02:47.356 --> 00:02:50.556
Double the number of turns while holding area, field and restoring constant

00:02:50.556 --> 00:02:50.976
fixed.

00:02:52.116 --> 00:02:53.196
Current sensitivity doubles.

00:02:53.896 --> 00:02:58.076
If the longer winding also doubles resistance, voltage sensitivity stays

00:02:58.076 --> 00:03:01.236
unchanged, because numerator and resistance both double.

00:03:02.096 --> 00:03:05.096
Greater current sensitivity alone does not guarantee greater voltage

00:03:05.096 --> 00:03:05.576
sensitivity.

00:03:06.496 --> 00:03:10.111
Keep coil resistance separate from the restoring constant, and keep radians

00:03:10.111 --> 00:03:10.256
per

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:12.156
ampere separate from radians per volt.

00:03:12.504 --> 00:03:16.044
A delicate galvanometer cannot carry a large current directly.

00:03:16.944 --> 00:03:21.364
To make an ammeter, put a small shunt resistance S in parallel with its coil.

00:03:22.144 --> 00:03:25.844
Most of the current uses the shunt, while a controlled small fraction passes

00:03:25.844 --> 00:03:26.704
through the galvanometer.

00:03:27.924 --> 00:03:31.564
Let full scale coil current be I g and coil resistance be G.

00:03:32.064 --> 00:03:36.799
If the desired full scale total current is I, the shunt current is I minus I

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:36.924
g.

00:03:36.924 --> 00:03:38.784
Parallel branches share the same voltage.

00:03:39.324 --> 00:03:43.284
So I g times G equals I minus I g, multiplied by S.

00:03:43.824 --> 00:03:48.624
Solve for S: I g G divided by I minus I g.

00:03:48.624 --> 00:03:52.044
Let's use a different instrument with full scale current one milliampere and

00:03:52.044 --> 00:03:53.484
coil resistance one hundred ohms.

00:03:54.104 --> 00:03:55.484
We want a one ampere range.

00:03:56.084 --> 00:04:00.284
At full scale the coil voltage is zero point one volt, and the shunt carries

00:04:00.284 --> 00:04:02.104
zero point nine nine nine amperes.

00:04:02.424 --> 00:04:06.444
The required shunt is approximately zero point one zero zero one ohms.

00:04:07.724 --> 00:04:11.624
The complete ammeter's resistance is voltage divided by total current, giving

00:04:11.624 --> 00:04:12.804
zero point one ohms.

00:04:13.924 --> 00:04:17.984
Do not round I minus I g to I until you know the approximation is acceptable.

00:04:18.704 --> 00:04:20.304
A second check is the circuit connection.

00:04:21.004 --> 00:04:24.624
The shunt is parallel to the coil inside the instrument, but the complete

00:04:24.624 --> 00:04:27.584
ammeter is connected in series with the branch whose current we measure.

00:04:28.344 --> 00:04:30.644
Those two statements refer to different parts of the circuit.

00:04:30.644 --> 00:04:35.644
We assume steady direct current, constant resistances and negligible lead

00:04:35.644 --> 00:04:36.964
resistance for these calculations.

00:04:37.464 --> 00:04:39.784
Now suppose we want to measure voltage.

00:04:40.744 --> 00:04:44.504
Connect a large resistance R in series with the coil to limit the current.

00:04:45.124 --> 00:04:48.944
The complete voltmeter is then connected across the two points whose potential

00:04:48.944 --> 00:04:49.764
difference we want.

00:04:50.524 --> 00:04:54.834
Same instrument: full scale coil current one milliampere, resistance one

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:55.364
hundred ohms.

00:04:55.984 --> 00:05:00.324
For a ten volt range, total resistance must be voltage divided by full scale

00:05:00.324 --> 00:05:02.144
current, or ten thousand ohms.

00:05:02.824 --> 00:05:07.244
The coil already contributes one hundred ohms, so the added series resistance

00:05:07.244 --> 00:05:09.284
is nine thousand nine hundred ohms.

00:05:10.104 --> 00:05:14.203
At full scale, the coil drops zero point one volt and the series resistor

00:05:14.203 --> 00:05:14.484
drops

00:05:14.484 --> 00:05:15.604
nine point nine volts.

00:05:16.304 --> 00:05:17.804
Together they make ten volts.

00:05:18.804 --> 00:05:21.584
Writing ten thousand ohms as the added resistance would count the coil

00:05:21.584 --> 00:05:22.464
resistance twice.

00:05:23.084 --> 00:05:26.744
In symbols, V equals I g times G plus R.

00:05:27.604 --> 00:05:30.704
Rearranging gives R equal to V over I g, minus G.

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:35.104
This design requires a target range at least as large as the bare coil's full

00:05:35.104 --> 00:05:38.924
scale voltage if we use only a nonnegative added series resistance.

00:05:39.644 --> 00:05:43.234
For a twenty volt range with the same coil, total resistance becomes twenty

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:46.884
thousand ohms and the added resistance becomes nineteen thousand nine hundred

00:05:46.884 --> 00:05:47.264
ohms.

00:05:48.104 --> 00:05:52.624
Remember the two levels of connection: multiplier and coil are internally in

00:05:52.624 --> 00:05:56.664
series, but the complete voltmeter is externally in parallel with the measured

00:05:56.664 --> 00:05:57.104
component.

00:05:57.744 --> 00:06:01.984
A voltmeter should draw little current, so high input resistance is desirable.

00:06:01.984 --> 00:06:06.044
It is still a real branch of the circuit, not an invisible observer.

00:06:06.312 --> 00:06:09.292
Let's see why meter resistance matters.

00:06:10.032 --> 00:06:12.352
An ideal ten volt source feeds a ten ohm resistor.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.652
Without a meter, current is one ampere.

00:06:16.192 --> 00:06:18.592
Insert our zero point one ohm ammeter in series.

00:06:19.372 --> 00:06:23.697
Total resistance becomes ten point one ohms, so current is about zero point

00:06:23.697 --> 00:06:25.112
nine nine zero amperes.

00:06:25.812 --> 00:06:29.092
The instrument correctly reads the current in the altered circuit, which is

00:06:29.092 --> 00:06:30.812
slightly smaller than the original current.

00:06:31.372 --> 00:06:32.542
Now a voltage example.

00:06:32.842 --> 00:06:37.042
An ideal ten volt source feeds two ten thousand ohm resistors in series.

00:06:37.722 --> 00:06:41.062
The voltage across the second resistor is five volts before measurement.

00:06:42.082 --> 00:06:44.642
Connect our ten thousand ohm voltmeter across that second resistor.

00:06:45.442 --> 00:06:49.022
Two equal ten thousand ohm branches in parallel give five thousand ohms.

00:06:49.802 --> 00:06:53.842
The loaded divider now has ten thousand ohms above and five thousand ohms

00:06:53.842 --> 00:06:54.162
below.

00:06:54.642 --> 00:06:59.280
Its measured voltage is ten times five thousand divided by fifteen thousand,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:06:59.402
or

00:06:59.402 --> 00:07:01.022
about three point three three volts.

00:07:01.962 --> 00:07:05.602
The drop from five volts is a loading effect, not a mistake in Ohm's law.

00:07:06.402 --> 00:07:09.382
A much higher voltmeter resistance would disturb this circuit less.

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:13.942
This is why an ideal ammeter has zero resistance and an ideal voltmeter has

00:07:13.942 --> 00:07:14.742
infinite resistance.

00:07:15.582 --> 00:07:19.096
Also, do not connect a low resistance ammeter directly across a voltage

00:07:19.096 --> 00:07:19.442
source;

00:07:19.842 --> 00:07:21.202
it can draw excessive current.

00:07:21.942 --> 00:07:26.846
For JEE problems, redraw the actual connections, preserve the coil's full

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:27.182
scale

00:07:27.182 --> 00:07:30.862
current, and use branch voltages and currents to derive the formulas.

00:07:31.682 --> 00:07:35.342
Our next class will turn from measuring instruments to magnetic dipole fields

00:07:35.342 --> 00:07:36.802
and bar magnet geometry.
